---
// Lesson 4.2a — Patrol Polish: Bounds, Flips & Animations
// The stuck-flip guard clauses, sprite flip, enemy animation.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.2a — Patrol Polish: Bounds, Flips &amp; Animations</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The starter's patrol ships with a strange pair of guard clauses
    that look redundant — they are not. Today you break the patrol the
    way a real bug would, meet the clause that saves it, and finish the
    enemy so it <em>reads</em> alive before it ever chases you.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Here is the full flip block from PlatformerMove — including the
    part that looks useless:</p>
    <pre><code>float dist = Vector3.Distance(startPosition, transform.position);
if (dist &gt; patrolDistance)
{
    direction *= -1; // Switch direction
    // Ensure we don't get stuck flipping
    if (direction == 1 &amp;&amp; transform.position.x &lt; startPosition.x) direction = 1;
    else if (direction == -1 &amp;&amp; transform.position.x &gt; startPosition.x) direction = -1;

    // Visual flip
    GetComponentInChildren&lt;SpriteRenderer&gt;().flipX = direction &lt; 0;
}</code></pre>

    <p>Predict the scenario that needs those middle clauses. Hint:
    Translate runs per frame; the enemy crosses the leash boundary
    somewhere. Where does it stand at the moment it flips — exactly at
    the boundary, or a little past it? Now imagine a BIG speed: each
    frame moves it a lot. Flip happens at frame N; frame N+1 moves
    again... how could that jitter at the edge?</p>

    <p>Then build the disease to see the cure: set speed to 15,
    patrolDistance to 0.1, and watch the enemy vibrate at the end of
    its beat. THAT is the stuck-flip the clauses prevent — flipping
    every frame while standing outside the leash.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The guard clauses say: "if I am heading right but I am
            actually LEFT of the anchor, commit to right." Explain in
            your own words what state the enemy was in when the naive
            flip failed — and why checking reality (position) beats
            trusting intent (direction).
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the visual flip.</strong> The last line reads
    the signed state you built in <a href="4.1a.html">4.1a</a> and
    mirrors the sprite: <code>flipX = direction &lt; 0</code>. Watch
    the face, not the movement: walking right shows the sprite's
    default, walking left mirrors it. One line of code, zero art
    changes — a mirror is cheaper than a second sprite.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — GetComponentInChildren, noticed.</strong> The
    flip line reaches the SpriteRenderer through
    GetComponentInChildren — the same call your PlayerController uses
    for its animator. Why "in children"? Because the visual sprite
    lives on a child object of the enemy prefab. Look at the MobileEnemy
    prefab in the Hierarchy: find which child carries the
    SpriteRenderer.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — make it breathe.</strong> The MobileEnemy ships
    with animation (there is a TopDown_EnemyAnimator in the starter's
    Package Scripts). Play with it: if the enemy walks with its own
    walk cycle, the patrol stops being a sliding statue. If its
    animator has an idle/walk parameter, wire the same trick the player
    used in <a href="1.6a.html">1.6a</a>: code sets state, animator
    plays frames.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Polish gone wrong</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Comment out the flipX line. The enemy moonwalks — what
                does the player actually see, and why is this the
                cheapest "broken" to fix?</li>
            <li>Change flipX to <code>direction &gt; 0</code>. Which
                direction faces correctly now, and which faces wrong?</li>
            <li>Delete BOTH guard clauses and set speed 15 /
                patrolDistance 0.1. Describe the vibration in frame
                terms: what does the enemy do each of the 60 frames
                per second?</li>
            <li>Flip the sprite by NEGATING the local scale instead
                (a classic alternative). Why can that break
                animations or colliders on some rigs — and why did
                flipX win?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Feel the guard</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Design a "look busy" idle: pause 0.5s at each end of
                the beat. What state do you add — and does the pause
                need its own timer field?</li>
            <li>Two enemies patrolling the SAME leash, half a beat
                apart. When the player watches them cross, what does
                the crossing look like — and can you make them
                collide?</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"4.2a: patrol polish + flip read"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Demonstrate the stuck-flip bug and explain the guard clauses that prevent it</li>
            <li>Explain flipX as a mirror driven by signed state</li>
            <li>Say what GetComponentInChildren does and why the enemy needs it</li>
            <li>Wire enemy animation the same way the player's works</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/index.html">Unit 4: The Enemy</a> — patrol, polish, chase</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — where this is heading</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.3a.html">4.3a — Chase: The Aggro Radius</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.2a" title="4.2a Patrol Polish: Bounds, Flips &amp; Animations — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
